CAT = "iepsaAccess"; SEG = "ACC"
# QQI 4N20604 learning outcomes 1.9, 1.15, 2.8, 2.9 (access and egress, electronic aids, confidential
# information, key handling); General Data Protection Regulation (EU) 2016/679 art. 5 principles and
# the Data Protection Act 2018 for CCTV footage (concepts only).
Q = [
("What is the main aim of access control on a site?",
 "Only authorised people enter", ["Count every car that passes the gate", "Sell passes to visitors", "Stop staff leaving early"],
 "Access control makes sure only people with a right to be there enter, and that you know who is on site, which matters for both crime and fire safety."),
("A visitor arrives for a meeting. What should a guard do before letting her through?",
 "Check her host expects her", ["Wave her in if well dressed", "Ask her to wait outside", "Let her find the office alone"],
 "Confirming the appointment with the host, recording the visit and issuing a visitor pass is standard. Appearance is no guide to authority."),
("Why must visitors sign in and out of the visitor log?",
 "To know who is on site", ["To collect their phone numbers", "To charge them for parking", "To check their spelling"],
 "An accurate log shows who is on site at any time, which is vital in an evacuation roll call and useful if an incident is investigated later."),
("A man in a high-visibility vest says he is from the electricity company and needs the plant room. What do you do?",
 "Check ID and call to confirm", ["Let him in, he has a vest", "Give him the master key", "Tell him to come back next year"],
 "Uniforms and vests are easy to obtain. Check photo ID and confirm the visit with the client or the company on a number you look up yourself, not one he gives you."),
('A staff member holds a swipe door open for a stranger to be helpful. What should the guard do?',
 'Politely check the stranger', ['Thank the staff member', 'Ignore it, it was polite', 'Lock the staff member out'],
 'Holding doors for strangers defeats access control. Check that the person is authorised or signed in, and remind staff of the rule without embarrassing anyone.'),
("A staff member reports her access card lost. What should happen first?",
 "Cancel the card on the system", ["Wait a week for it to turn up", "Issue a card with her PIN", "Leave the door unlocked"],
 "Deactivating a lost card at once stops anyone who finds it from using it. A replacement is issued under the site's procedure and the loss is recorded."),
("What is the purpose of a key register?",
 "Record who holds each key", ["List every lock's maker", "Show where spare bulbs are", "Note staff birthdays"],
 "A key register records each key's issue and return with the name, time and signature, so every key can be accounted for at any time."),
("A contractor asks to borrow the master key overnight. What should you do?",
 "Refuse unless authorised", ["Lend it if he is polite", "Copy it for him first", "Leave it under the mat"],
 "Master keys open many doors and should never leave the site or be issued without the authorisation the site instructions require. Unauthorised issue is a serious breach."),
("At the end of a shift, a key in the register is shown as signed out but not returned. What do you do?",
 "Report it, follow it up", ["Sign it back in yourself", "Ignore it until tomorrow", "Remove that page from it"],
 "An unreturned key is a risk to the site. Contact the holder or the supervisor, record the action and never falsify the register."),
("Why should key tags not show the name of the building or room the key opens?",
 "A lost key can't be traced", ["Tags wear out faster", "It is against the law", "Long names are hard to read"],
 "Coded tags, with the code list kept secure, mean that a lost or stolen key cannot easily be matched to the lock it opens."),
("Which document lists a site's security duties, procedures and contacts for each post?",
 "The assignment instructions", ["The visitor log book", "The incident report form for the site", "The weekly staff rota"],
 "Assignment instructions (sometimes called site or post orders) tell each guard what to do at that site, including patrol routes, access rules, contacts and emergency procedures."),
("A caller says she is the client's manager and asks for the alarm code. What should you do?",
 "Refuse and verify the caller", ["Give it, she is the boss", "Read it out slowly to her on the line", "Text it to her mobile"],
 "Codes and passwords are never given out over the phone. Verify the caller through the agreed procedure and report the attempt, which may be social engineering."),
("Under the GDPR principles, how long should CCTV footage be kept?",
 "No longer than needed", ["Forever, just in case", "Exactly 10 years", "Until the disk fills"],
 "Storage limitation in Article 5 of the GDPR means personal data, including CCTV images, is kept no longer than necessary for its purpose. Sites set a short retention period and keep footage longer only for an incident."),
("A shopper asks a guard for a copy of CCTV footage showing her. What is the right response?",
 "Refer her to the data controller", ["Hand her the recorder now", "Refuse, the footage is secret", "Post it online for her"],
 "People have a right of access to their personal data, but requests go to the data controller (usually the client) under its procedure. A guard does not hand over footage himself."),
("Why should a CCTV monitor not face where the public can see it?",
 "Images are personal data", ["The screen gets too hot", "It can confuse drivers", "It wastes electricity"],
 "CCTV images of identifiable people are personal data, so access to them is limited to authorised staff."),
("A guard discusses a client's alarm problems with friends in a pub. What has he breached?",
 "Confidentiality", ["Equality law", "His fire duties", "Trespass law"],
 "Information about a client's security weaknesses is confidential and could be used by criminals. Guards discuss site matters only with people who need to know."),
("Which is a good way to handle a confidential document left on a desk during a night patrol?",
 "Secure it and report it", ["Read it carefully", "Take it home to keep safe", "Throw it in the bin"],
 "Securing the document, for example in a locked drawer or by handing it to the supervisor, and recording it in the report protects the client without the guard reading it."),
("Where should a guard keep the site's alarm codes and passwords?",
 "Only where procedures allow", ["Written on his hand", "On a sticky note on the screen", "In his phone's social media notes"],
 "Codes are kept only where the assignment instructions allow, never written where others can see them."),
("A vehicle arrives at a gate barrier. What should the guard check under typical site rules?",
 "Driver, purpose and pass", ["Only the car's colour", "The radio station playing", "Nothing, cars are fine"],
 "Vehicle access control checks who the driver is, why they are there and whether they hold a pass or booking, and often records the registration and time."),
("Why do sites have one main entrance for visitors rather than many open doors?",
 "Easier to control entry", ["It saves on cleaning", "It saves on lighting", "It makes queues longer"],
 "Funnelling visitors through one controlled entrance lets a guard check everyone, while other doors stay secure but available as fire exits."),
("A guard finds a fire exit propped open with a bin. What should he do?",
 "Close it and report it", ["Leave it open for airflow", "Lock it with a chain", "Put a warning sign on it"],
 "A propped fire door lets intruders in and lets fire and smoke spread. Close it, report it and never chain or lock a fire exit, which must stay usable."),
("What is an electronic aid commonly used in access control?",
 "Card reader", ["Fire blanket", "First aid kit", "Hi-vis jacket"],
 "Card readers, keypads, intercoms, turnstiles and biometric readers are electronic aids that control or record who passes through a door."),
("An employee leaves the company. What should happen to her access rights?",
 "Removed when she leaves", ["Kept active for a year", "Passed to her friend", "Left exactly as they are"],
 "Access rights should end when employment ends, and keys and cards are returned, so a former employee cannot get in later."),
('Why should contractors working on a site wear a visible pass?',
 'Shows they are authorised', ['It keeps their clothes clean', 'It is needed for tax', 'It lets them skip queues'],
 'A visible pass lets staff and guards see at a glance that a person has been signed in and is allowed to be there. Anyone without one can be politely challenged.'),
]
